What was the problem you were solving in the projects for this course?

The problem that I was solving in the projects was to make a recommendation of a data structure to use to store course data from a CSV file for Computer Science Department at ABCU and then implement that data structure into code that the department could use for their students and their course schedules.  I first create pseudocode for displaying a menu that would allow the user to perform various operations on the data structure depending on their input.  I then analyzed the performance and runtime of the pseudocode that I created and made a recommendation on what data structure would best suit their needs.  Based on that recommendation, I then created a program that would perform the tasks that ABCU wanted using the data strucure I recommended.


How did you approach the problem? Consider why data structures are important to understand.

First, I studied and reviewed the pros and cons of the different data structures to determine what would be best for ABCU and be able to meet their needs.  While some structures may be better than others for accomplishing certain tasks, I felt that a Hash table
or a BST would be the best options to accomplish what ABCU was wanting.  A BST was what I ended up going with when coding the project.


How did you overcome any roadblocks you encountered while going through the activities or project?

My two main roadblocks that I came across were related to the analysis of the data structures for Project One and the implementation of a hash table for the code.  For some reason, the analysis of the runtime of the different functions didn't seem to click right away so I had to watch a few videos to help me get a better understanding of what the ask was.  Upon reviewing my pseudocode for the implementation of a BST and a Hash Table, I found that I included a lot of unneccesary steps in the BST to where that made it a lot less efficient than a Hash table would be so I pivoted Project Two to be working with a BST compared to a hashtable as I had a significantly easier time using that data structure.


How has your work on this project expanded your approach to designing software and developing programs?

My work on this project has expanded my approach to designing software and developing programs in a few ways.  
One way is that I am more focused on the user experience when they use the program/software and what it looks like when they are using it.  For so long, I would just focus on getting a program to work and do what it was supposed to do and that was it.
Now, however, I am more focuses on what the user expereinces when using it.  Because of this, I made changes that would make the output of the program a little bit more appealing compared to how I have done it in the past.


How has your work on this project evolved the way you write programs that are maintainable, readable, and adaptable?

Throughout the course and even my time here at SNHU, my biggest area of improvment has been creating programs that are modular instead of trying to lump all tasks needed into a small amount of functions.  My pseudocode and even my actual code would have a small amount of functions but each function would be extremely large and would be a bit of a pain to have to maintain and change.  One change that I made between the beginning of the course and the end was to make my functions more modular and have more functions that perform very specific tasks.  By doing this, even though it forced me to have more functions in my code, it made my program easier to read, easier to maintain, and easier to adapt to any new changes that I needed to implement.

